I'll search the codebase for the verdict parser implementation.**File: `/Users/nick/Desktop/vigil/src/plan/verdict.ts`**

### Exposed function

```ts
export function parseVerdictLines(text: string): VerdictLine[]
```

It takes a block of text, splits it on `\r?\n`, and returns every recognized verdict line as a structured object.

### What it recognizes

The `VerdictLine` union has three variants, matched by three regexes in priority order:

| Order | Regex | Shape | Example line |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `PLAN_RE` | `{ type: "plan", planId, verdict }` | `PLAN my-plan: PASS` |
| 2 | `GATE_RE` | `{ type: "gate", gateId, verdict, reason? }` | `VG-BUILD-01: FAIL missing artifact` |
| 3 | `TASK_RE` | `{ type: "task", taskId }` | `TASK 003-evidence: DONE` |

Notable details:
- All patterns tolerate surrounding markdown noise via `[`*_]*` — so `` `VG-X: PASS` `` or `**PLAN p: PASS**` still parse.
- All are case-insensitive; `gateId` is normalized to uppercase and `verdict` to lowercase.
- Gate `reason` is the trailing remainder, trimmed of leading separators (`-`, `–`, `—`, `:`), and becomes `undefined` if empty.
- The `continue` after each match means one line yields at most one verdict, and `PLAN` wins over `VG-` which wins over `TASK`.

### Companion export

The same file also exports `LineBuffer`, a small stateful splitter for streamed output — `push(chunk)` returns complete lines and retains the partial tail; `flush()` drains it. This is what lets the runner parse verdicts incrementally from the SDK stream rather than waiting for the full message.

### Consumers

`src/engine/runner.ts` and `src/engine/session.ts` are the main users, with coverage in `tests/parser.test.ts`, `tests/verdict-rails.test.ts`, and `tests/session-verdict.test.ts`.

One observation: `GATE_RE` hardcodes the `VG-` prefix. If you ever want project-configurable gate ID prefixes (plausible alongside the planned `vigil init` scaffolding work), that regex becomes the place to parameterize.
